<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    ONE PROJECT, NOT FOUR - and this is a deliberate architectural decision,
    not laziness.

    Catalog got Domain / Application / Infrastructure / Api because it has real
    rules worth isolating and it was the cheapest place to learn the layout.
    A basket is a per-user document with almost no invariants: quantity must be
    at least one, and you cannot hoard a thousand distinct line items. Splitting
    that across four assemblies would produce four files of pass-through code
    and three project references to justify them.

    Clean Architecture is a RESPONSE TO COMPLEXITY, not a default. The layers
    still exist here - they are folders (Domain, Persistence, Baskets,
    Controllers) rather than assemblies. The boundary is visible; it just is not
    enforced by the compiler. That is the trade-off, and at this size it is the
    right one.

    If Basket grows real rules - pricing, promotions, reservations - we split
    it, and by then you will have FELT the reason rather than assumed it.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="10.0.12" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="10.0.12" />
    <PackageReference Include="Swashbuckle.AspNetCore" Version="10.2.3" />

    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="10.0.12">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\BuildingBlocks\CommerceFlow.BuildingBlocks.Authentication\CommerceFlow.BuildingBlocks.Authentication.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Note what is NOT here: a reference to Catalog. Basket stores a ProductId and
    a copy of the name and price, and has no idea what a Catalog.Domain.Product
    is. In Phase 5 it will ASK Catalog over HTTP - still without referencing it.
  -->

</Project>
